matroskadec: forget an end of stream after a generic or byte seek

The demuxer latches end of stream in MatroskaDemuxContext.done, set by
matroska_resync() and the end-of-segment check. Only
matroska_read_seek() clears it, but a seek can also bypass it:
AVSEEK_FLAG_BYTE goes straight to seek_frame_byte(), and a failed
index seek falls back to seek_frame_generic(). Both only move the IO
position and set io_repositioned, so once the file had been read to
its end, or a read had failed, every later read returned AVERROR_EOF
at once, wherever the seek had landed (AetherEngine issue 944: a
subtitle side reader whose positioning seek was cut by a deadline
fell back to a byte estimate and read EOF at second 0 of an 888 s
file). On n8.1.3, reading any file to EOF and byte seeking back to 0
reads no packets at all.

read_packet now honours io_repositioned the way oggdec and av1dec do:
it resets the parsing state at the new position, drops the queue and
a pending keyframe skip, and clears done. matroska_read_seek() clears
io_repositioned, so the state it sets up is not reset again.

--- a/libavformat/matroskadec.c
+++ b/libavformat/matroskadec.c
@@ -4345,6 +4345,17 @@
 {
     MatroskaDemuxContext *matroska = s->priv_data;
     int ret = 0;
+
+    if (s->io_repositioned) {
+        /* A generic or byte seek moved the read position without
+         * matroska_read_seek(): parse from the new position, and forget an
+         * end of stream reached before it. */
+        s->io_repositioned = 0;
+        matroska_reset_status(matroska, 0, -1);
+        matroska_clear_queue(matroska);
+        matroska->skip_to_keyframe = 0;
+        matroska->done             = 0;
+    }
 
     if (matroska->resync_pos == -1) {
         // This can only happen if generic seeking has been used.
@@ -4415,6 +4426,7 @@
     }
     matroska->skip_to_keyframe = 1;
     matroska->done             = 0;
+    s->io_repositioned         = 0;
     avpriv_update_cur_dts(s, st, sti->index_entries[index].timestamp);
     return 0;
 err:
